1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. Every request to Claude shares one context window, and it's a finite token

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
budget you manage yourself, not something the model tidies up for you. This lesson covers

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
what fills that window, why nothing is dropped automatically, and how drift and bloat creep

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
into a long session. Let's lock down three concepts the rest of the lesson builds on.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
First, the context window. That's the maximum number of tokens the model can attend to

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
in one request, spanning the system prompt, tool definitions, history, tool results,

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
and the current generation. Everything the model reasons over has to fit inside it.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Second, context window management. That's the discipline of deliberately controlling

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,000
what occupies that budget so the highest signal content stays in view. It's an active

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,000
engineering task, not a model-managed behavior. Third, the attention budget. Think of it like

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
working memory. Every new token you add depletes it, so more context isn't automatically more

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
capable. So what actually eats that budget? It helps to picture everything that lands in the

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
window on a single request. Your system prompt sits there on every call, so do all your tool

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
definitions. And if you're wired up to MCP servers, their schemas too. Then there's the

15
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
whole conversation history. Every prior user turn and assistant reply. On top of that, every tool

16
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
result you fed back, and those raw dumps are usually the biggest single source. And finally, the

17
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
response being generated right now. Add it up and you'll see why a session that felt small at the

18
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
start can quietly balloon. Knowing the pieces is the first step to trimming them. Here's the

19
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
assumption that burns people. Many developers expect the model to quietly forget stale turns

20
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and keep only what matters. The way a person lets old details fade. That's not what happens.

21
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Context accumulation is additive. Nothing is dropped unless your application prunes or compacts

22
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
it. The model doesn't choose to trim and the API doesn't summarize on your behalf when the window

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
fills. So the reality is the opposite of the assumption. An unmanaged long session doesn't

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
degrade because the model got tired or forgot. It degrades because you kept appending and the

25
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
useful signal got buried under everything you never cleared out. Ownership sits with you,

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:29,000
not the model. Let's put names to why a stuffed window hurts. The first is context rot. As the

27
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
number of tokens climbs, the model's ability to accurately recall any one fact from that context

28
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
goes down. A buried instruction competes with thousands of low value tokens around it. The

29
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,000
second is the attention budget. Under the hood, the model relates every token to every other token.

30
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
So cost and dilution rise faster than the token count alone suggests. Put those together and the

31
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
lesson is uncomfortable but useful. A bigger context isn't a free upgrade. Past a point,

32
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,000
piling on more tokens actively works against recall and focus, which is exactly why tight

33
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
beats large. Let me name the three ways a long session goes wrong because you'll diagnose all

34
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
three by feel eventually. The first is drift. The model gradually loses the thread as tangents and

35
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
stale results dilute your instructions and it starts reverting to earlier assumptions.

36
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
The second is bloat. The window fills with low value tokens, verbose tool dumps,

37
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
and redundant history, which raises cost and latency and crowds out room to reason.

38
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
The third is overflow. You simply run past the token limit and that's a hard error you have

39
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:49,000
to prevent because the API won't auto summarize to save you. Drift, bloat, overflow. Different

40
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
symptoms, same root cause, an unmanaged window. Let's slow down on drift because the fix is

41
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
counterintuitive. When the model starts ignoring a recent constraint or sliding back to an old

42
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
assumption, the instinct is to scold it, to add a firmer, louder instruction on top. That usually

43
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
makes things worse because you just added more tokens to an already diluted window.

44
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Drift is a composition problem, not a model defect. The real fix is to reground and prune,

45
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
restate the current goal cleanly, and cut the stale turns and dead tool output that are drowning

46
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
it out. You're not writing a stronger prompt, you're rebuilding a cleaner context so the

47
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
instruction that matters carries weight again. Three more terms round out the vocabulary. First,

48
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
high signal context. The goal is never to fit everything you could, it's to keep the smallest

49
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
set of tokens that still drives the outcome. Anthropic frames it as keeping context informative

50
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
yet tight. Second, regrounding. That's the move we just described, restating the goal and the

51
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:02,000
constraints that matter. So a diluted instruction gets its weight back. Third, windowing. Instead

52
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
of carrying the entire history forever, you keep a recent slice and let older turns fall away. It's

53
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
a bounded, simple alternative to summarizing, and for many agents it's enough on its own to hold

54
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:21,000
the line. So how do you actually work this into your build? Treat the context window as something

55
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:28,000
you curate on purpose, every turn, not something you fill and forget. Before you append a giant

56
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
tool result, ask what the model genuinely needs from it downstream, and keep only that. When you

57
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
notice the thread slipping, reground instead of nagging, and decide up front how history is bounded,

58
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
whether that's windowing, pruning, or a summary pass we'll cover next. The through line is a single

59
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:54,000
principle. Find the smallest set of high signal tokens that gets the job done. Informative yet

60
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
tight. That mindset separates an agent that scales from one that slowly chokes on its own history.

61
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Let's recap. The context window is a finite budget you own, and nothing in it is dropped

62
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:14,000
automatically. That's why drift and bloat come from an unmanaged window, not a tired model.

63
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
One thing to watch, the fix for drift is regrounding and pruning, not a louder prompt.

64
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:24,000
That covers managing the context window in code. I'll see you in the next one.

